#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7c99605a-2d55-5d37-b363-2e33fa02575c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10456" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =1-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =3(1-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(3(1-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =9(1-x)^{2} \\\\ y & =9(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 9(x - 1)^2.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function Containing a Linear Argument"}] :question/id #uuid "8f01f2b7-13d5-58d3-8e2e-9f6804993094" :question/math-academy-id "e-10457" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y + 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{4y+1} & =5x \\\\ {(\\sqrt{4y+1})}^{2} & ={(5x)}^{2} \\\\ 4y+1 & =25{x}^{2} \\\\ 4y & =25{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}(25{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(25x^2 - 1\\right).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq -\\dfrac14, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq -\\dfrac14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10be7535-989f-5706-964e-06c02251d1f5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8760afc0-a7fb-5398-a585-c5559c9d1bb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10458" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x + k} - 2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 + 3.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x+k} - 2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y + k} - 2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y+k}-2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y+k} & =x+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+2) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x+2))}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+2)^{2}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 - k.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k = 3 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = -3.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3, \\qquad f(x) \\geq -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f689c3e3-9bb9-5e1e-b69a-b691373fdee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10496" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function ![](images/b9/b9a5eece426fa2aa66aac43b9afaf2812dcf483795a84d5e96fa89c0dcd71db6.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/6e/6e9ea2da83f324dd20158e4b08fb1c6968b6387d458964f21ed03648be954752.svg) we start by replacing ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) with ![](images/02/028bcadf67e469d96df97e89362f88bb8edca3ddea5579906987cc124663fba8.svg)\n![](images/d4/d4f5098eaa3eb85b97ec9547373f138922d22d734018ede816930c665248c0d5.svg)\n\nNow, interchanging ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) gives\n![](images/35/35c51b7dff42eeb3c3b2033c78495c07294048ba02628e583382c0807e43d192.svg)\n\nThen, we make ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject of the equation, as follows:\n![](images/18/183e560a55c159a77b1ceacec766572e2279ad4e552242d638a9fdcf96f2c859.svg)\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n![](images/b5/b55f51d46d8917e5a44e4dae697c71a004580924cecd5a5f079e5eac7f480b3a.svg)\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) are\n![](images/f2/f2f23ba499fcbb1dee1964f08453be99bdf5ed7ec11581b773845c4aa307b64a.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ![](images/90/90bf8beda1404c3f6e9d9d9565922945fb59df9b0488050e3d2b72a153addd3c.svg) are\n![](images/96/96d9c5432e8f14ac6d9bc1841e37772cad060e3d09065129f73161726aeeffd2.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function Containing a Linear Argument"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b5ccdc-248f-5494-be14-486777a3d0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)^{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555008} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca8e067-88c2-59dc-9212-7b349198dbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555020} {:answer/id #uuid "646768b3-6e7f-503c-ba19-015971768b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(2x-4)^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555227} {:answer/id #uuid "791d0095-f94d-537e-8cbc-ca34d90cec5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=(2x+4)^{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555279} {:answer/id #uuid "c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56d5d82-af0a-539a-a09a-91f862a9a437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555477} :answer-field/id #uuid "a75f529d-a4ae-53d6-b5b2-1258fe33e96c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d170561d-a12f-5a5e-8c7d-c28c8d751c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-148290" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{2x+3}-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{2x+3}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+3} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y+3})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y+3 & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y & =(x+4)^{3}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function Containing a Linear Argument"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555152} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{4{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555447} {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} {:answer/id #uuid "df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{3}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555545} {:answer/id #uuid "fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{3}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} :answer-field/id #uuid "42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148557" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-3} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y-3})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{3}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10be7535-989f-5706-964e-06c02251d1f5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} {:answer/id #uuid "62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555218} {:answer/id #uuid "a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555386} {:answer/id #uuid "d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555524} {:answer/id #uuid "e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} :answer-field/id #uuid "02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-148573" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5,\\,x>-k\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-2,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{2}{3(x-5)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\nk=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ccc9616-d536-5517-8214-1ef54b80d911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555329} {:answer/id #uuid "980ba604-0d0c-5c12-b156-6bec05449b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555363} {:answer/id #uuid "9db8fff9-fc74-5384-890d-71e40b070419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8{x}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555380} {:answer/id #uuid "dec3e383-be88-58cc-a27f-e0a2319a7eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x+3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555541} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9fdc19-4248-52ad-8f82-73729afa7ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555623} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43f1807-b8f6-5338-8144-d4033ce49512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56a829fb-a840-50d6-85ba-f7cb746a9aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-148734" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =2(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(2(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =8(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2395b540-2627-5ab1-83d5-405723b43cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27-8{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555071} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd61b85-6adb-58d6-bd60-2cc7be863377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555166} {:answer/id #uuid "755b6adb-f545-5977-ab1a-a3249a50e72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555266} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c6a5b9-c9bf-5d05-847f-e7d34bd69641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27(x+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555522} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c89a0-5bf5-5ae6-aa81-3cadda0a58cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555632} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c160c4-3699-526a-aa75-85488137bfed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16fdfda4-c2d1-55b9-ac4e-fb5a2493ec8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-148735" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =-\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(-\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{9}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555178} {:answer/id #uuid "63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555223} {:answer/id #uuid "8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{8(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555319} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8}{3(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148745" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ x-5 & =-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<5,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189} :answer-field/id #uuid "75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-270765" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10be7535-989f-5706-964e-06c02251d1f5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f0d53b4-cbce-5197-b2da-9a7671c36787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b029e56d-3e32-5c49-bf49-62786ef1d0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03af7723-aeb4-55f1-8ed6-486c6b158e90" :question/math-academy-id "q-277843" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=32(2-x)^{3}-2.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =k(2-x) \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y+4})}^{3} & ={(k(2-x))}^{3} \\\\ 2y+4 & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3} \\\\ 2y & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3}-4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}=32\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$"}]}
